[Music]  time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week with CBS  News chief medical correspondent dr. Jon  LaPook and CBS News contributor dr. Tara  Narula we begin with two new studies out  this week examining the role of exercise  and nutrition in recovery from colon  cancer John looks at the details  five years ago Dave strong and his wife  Mary decided to get in shape they  changed their diet and hit the gym five  days a week after turning fifty last  year strong had his first colonoscopy  the first thing I saw was my wife over  me with tears in her eyes so I knew  immediately something was bad the bad  news was colon cancer that had already  spread to his liver he's had three  surgeries and is about to start his last  five treatments of chemo I push myself  when I could I slowed down when I had to  but I tried to keep the normalcy going  he continues to eat healthy and work out  new research suggests those kinds of  lifestyle choices can help one study  followed patients with colon cancer who  had completed treatment researchers  scored them based on body weight  physical activity and dietary choices  those with the highest scores had a 42%  lower chance of dying over seven years  dr. Charles Fuchs at the Yale Cancer  Center co-authored both studies the diet  probably changed your body's chemistry  in a way that makes the growth of cancer  far more hostile in another study colon  cancer patients who ate at least two  servings of tree nuts a week had a 46%  lower chance of relapse over seven years  tree nuts like almonds walnuts and  cashews helped control insulin levels  and it's believed insulin can fuel the  growth of colon cancer cells by taking  nuts you reduce your risk your diabetes  and for colon cancer patients you reduce  the likelihood of the cancer coming back  wow that's fascinating the just  fascinate is so interesting that we're  not talking about pills here we're  talking about lifestyles exercise things  like that and diet and actually these  kind of studies don't prove cause and  effect but they do make sense from a  scientific point of view the  that if you could drive down insulin  levels in the body it might make a  difference yeah I found out something I  didn't know in the lab when they're  trying to make colon cancer cells grow  they add insulin Wow Wow eat your tree  nuts yes our next topic vaccinations and  international travel most measles  infections in the u.s. are the result of  travelers returning home to this country  a study published this week in the  annals of internal medicine looked at  travelers headed overseas and how  frequently they refused vaccinations for  the measles mumps and rubella prior to  traveling the data included adults who  visit a network of clinics that focus on  international travel health over 6500  people were eligible for the vaccine at  the time of their clinic visits however  over half did not get vaccinated during  the consultation tower what were the  reasons for that 48% of it was travelers  refusing mostly because of lack of  concern 28% was provider decision saying  they don't need it usually and about 24%  were health system barriers so for  instance the clinic would send them back  to their primary care to get vaccinated  I think the biggest issue is really  educating travelers that this is a  travel related illness I don't think  that people think about measles mumps  and rubella is something that they could  get abroad but in fact measles for  example has been eliminated in this  country since 2000 but we still have  outbreaks these outbreaks if you trace  them back they are coming from an  imported source over half of these cases  that are coming back are in u.s.  travelers who were unvaccinated so very  important to get that word out important  to get these vaccinations before you go  abroad especially not only to protect  your own health but those of those in  this country who are don't have the  immunity to fight off so they are  immunosuppressed or babies for instance  who are too young to get vaccinated John  did the study drill down into that  resistance and how to convince patients  yeah well Tara mentioned most of it  about three-quarters of the people just  were not concerned about the illness is  no big deal and it's true measles can  come and go but it can also cause  serious problems like encephalitis and  even death and as Tara said even if  you're not concerned about yourself you  have to be concerned about the other  people and you said it beautifully  which is you can bring it into another  community that's just happened in  Minnesota somebody came back possibly  but somehow there was a group of people  there who were unvaccinated and they got  the measles there are people who can't  get it and again I go to the baby that  you don't get the first shot until  you're about 12 months old right so what  happens between then and birth you can  get it if somebody comes back whatever  right what are the current  recommendations Tara if you're traveling  in terms of the vaccine yeah so the CDC  has a website they do have  recommendations and that is essentially  that for teenagers or adults who do not  have immunity they should get two doses  of the vaccine separated by about 28  days so to know if you're immune or not  if you have a blood test that shows  antibodies you would be immune if you  were born before 1957 when there were  lots of measles cases you would be mewn  or if you've already had measles for  instance when it comes to infants if you  have an infant and you're going to  travel before they're one year old they  should get a shot between six and 11  months and then two more doses after the  age of one against separated by 28 days  you know this is a very infectious  disease if people are not immunized and  they're exposed over ninety percent of  them are going to get this infection and  the vaccine is very effective 97%  effective against measles about 88%  effective against mumps finally quitting  smoking can be a major challenge but a  little encouragement can go a long way a  new study by the American Cancer Society  looked at the role that emails can play  in helping people quit the study  included around a thousand smokers who  were planning to stop it found that the  group that received the most personally  tailored emails had the highest  abstinence rate followed by those who  got the second most and then those who  received only one non tailored email so  what I'm learning from this Tara is stay  in touch with your friends who are  trying to quit smoking yes you know I  think they comment about how it's that  personalized messaging that makes you  feel like somebody cares that somebody  wants you to succeed and we know that  just in general people who tend to be  married who have strong social support  systems there studies that show they  live longer they have less  cardiovascular disease so that network  of support even if it comes in the form  of an email is important I'm wondering  what the typical email is supposed to  say well well don't give up yeah I think  they'll surrender  the technical medical term for what you  need in your life is a nudge nudge works  well community helps for all kinds of  health outcomes right dr. strong LaPook  and tara Narula thanks as always

